---
import TopicPage from '../../../../components/TopicPage.astro';
import Widget from '../../../../components/Widget.astro';

const awCfg = {
  curves: [
    { expr: 'exp(-6*(x-0.9)^2) + 0.04', label: 'spoilage rate', color: '#dc2626', width: 2.4 },
    { expr: '0.55*exp(-14*(x-0.99)^2)', label: 'lipid oxidation', color: '#b45309', width: 2.0 },
  ],
  x: { min: 0, max: 1, label: 'water activity (a-w)' },
  y: { min: 0, max: 1.2, label: 'relative rate (a.u.)' },
  crossings: false,
  height: 340,
};

const awStepsCfg = {
  steps: [
    { math: '1 · Water content ≠ water activity', note: 'A food can be wet and stable (jam, ~30% water) or dry and perishable (fresh meat, ~70%... no — meat is wet AND perishable). The key measure is not how much water, but how available it is.' },
    { math: '2 · Water activity defined', note: 'a-w = the food\'s equilibrium humidity, from 0 (bone dry) to 1.0 (pure water). It measures how tightly the matrix holds its water.' },
    { math: '3 · Microbes have thresholds', note: 'Most bacteria need a-w above ~0.91; yeasts tolerate to ~0.88; moulds to ~0.80. Below those lines, they simply cannot grow.' },
    { math: '4 · Preservation is a-w control', note: 'Salt, sugar and drying all work the same way: they bind water and drop the a-w. Jam, jerky and honey are the same idea at different sweetness.' },
    { math: '5 · But chemistry has its own curve', note: 'Lipid oxidation peaks near the dry end — the reason crackers go stale-rancid even while microbially safe. Every target a-w is a compromise.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/food-science/foundational/food-chemistry-fundamentals">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Food science begins with a chemical re-framing: a meal is a matrix of water, carbohydrates,
    proteins and lipids — and its shelf life, texture and taste all follow from how those molecules
    interact. The single most useful number in the discipline is not how much water food contains
    but how <strong>available</strong> that water is: water activity, a-w.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="water activity, step by step" widget="equationStep" config={awStepsCfg} />

  <h2>One curve explains the pantry</h2>
  <p>
    Microbial spoilage rises as water becomes available, peaking near a-w of 0.9 and collapsing
    below the microbial thresholds. Lipid oxidation — the stale, cardboard rancidity of dry foods —
    does the opposite, accelerating when surface fats are exposed but dehydrated. Every preserved
    food lives in the gap between those two curves.
  </p>

  <Widget no="2" caption="why jam keeps but crackers go rancid" widget="plotter" config={awCfg} />

  <h2>The macronutrients as materials</h2>
  <p>
    Carbohydrates, proteins and lipids are not just nutrition here — they are structural materials.
    Starch granules gelatinise and retrograde (why bread stales), proteins unfold and knit into
    networks (why egg sets and yoghurt gels), and fats crystallise into polymorphs (why chocolate
    snaps or blooms). Texture is applied polymer science.
  </p>

  <h2>Flavour is chemistry too</h2>
  <p>
    Taste is receptor chemistry (sweet, salty, sour, bitter, umami), but much of what we call
    flavour arrives retronasally from volatile molecules — and many of the best ones are reaction
    products. The Maillard reaction between reducing sugars and amino acids builds hundreds of
    aroma compounds; it is why toast, roast coffee and seared steak share a family resemblance.
    Control the chemistry, control the craving.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> IFT undergraduate education standards; Belitz, Grosch &amp; Schieberle, <em>Food Chemistry</em>; Labuza's classic work on water activity and shelf life.</p>
</TopicPage>
